rt of that struggle is finding  a combat style that works for you and  sticking with it whether it's a guns  blazing or more tactical approach enemy  variety and multiple paths through any  given area means you'll be rewarded with  a thrilling fight regardless of how you  decide to tackle it you'll also be  rewarded with greater effectiveness in  combat at a steady pace successfully  executing a certain number of stealth  takedowns for example unlocks a perk  that increases your movement speed while  crouched and using upgrade parts you can  occasionally find lying around to say  put a silencer on your pistol will  further improve your stealth ability  but you're afforded the flexibility to  decide mid-mission that stealth is not  going to work and change tactics as a  result every encounter is incredibly  tense since you never know when you're  going to need to take it slow or book it  to safety as bullets fly by  [Music]  the far future technology of the Nazi  regime is both exhilarating to partaken  and a row test display of their ruthless  subjugation of all corners of the world  high-powered laser weapons are exciting  to use but the armored machine men who  dropped them are a reminder of human  experimentation during and after the war  anything is possible in Wolfenstein and  that's a direct result of immense human  suffering some particularly punishing  fights or a disadvantageous autosave can  be frustrating but most missions in just  before that frustration can turn to  anger aside from being a much-needed  break from fighting the story cutscenes  are beautifully directed and showcase  the series fantastic characters wait yes  streets are quiet each person on the  u-boat has their own story of oppression  and marginalization from the Black  Panthers to general angles anti-nazi  daughter even when the story goes  completely off the rails in an  absolutely jaw-dropping way there's  still room to explore their individual  dynamics and it balances the heavy  subject matter with a cheeky gung-ho  attitude that keeps you invested in the  crew and the cause anything  let's force more gays in there are times  when the new Colossus overreaches for  poignancy and as a result it states that  seems too overtly instead of letting  them stand alone some elements of  American patriotism like lines about  liberty and freedom feel misguided after  flashbacks that show the rampant racism  in the America Vijay's childhood and  while the arguments over the purpose of  the war and inspirational speeches about  fighting against impossible odds show  the breadth and depth of the resistance  movement for the most part these are  things you can discern just from playing  normally of course you should keep  fighting the Nazis even when it seems  pointless after all overcoming their and  stubble what might mission after mission  is satisfying enough on its own to keep  you going  again the main point here is killing  Nazis and no-questions-asked and the new  Colossus never lets you forget who and  why you're fighting Nazi brutality is on  full display from the blown out  irradiated remains of Manhattan to each  of the resistance members who all carry  mental scars if not physical ones  you're never given enough time between  cutscene missions and even downtime on  the u-boat to lose sight of the Reich's  cruelty Wolfenstein tense gameplay  elevates this further by giving you the  power to truly resist even when it all  seems hopeless  [Music]  [Music]
 

